  "enhanced_information": "The development of female internal genitalia is inhibited by the presence of anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH), which is secreted by Sertoli cells. This hormone specifically inhibits the development of the Müllerian ducts, which are precursors to female internal structures. Leydig cells produce testosterone, which promotes the development of male internal genitalia but does not directly inhibit female structures. The roles of the allantois and syncytiotrophoblast are not related to the inhibition of female genitalia. Therefore, the correct structure responsible for the inhibition of female internal genitalia is the Sertoli cells, as they secrete AMH that prevents the formation of female internal structures."
